00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
today's guitar tip is brought to you by  arpeggianto my online music school  specializing in all things that go pluck  to celebrate the new year we're offering  20 off of your first pack of lessons  just use code happy 2022 before the end  of january  hey guys welcome back for another guitar  tip  today's tip is going to be all about  keeping your instruments safe from  damage  and specifically damage due to humidity  you know it's so easy to look at a  guitar and you know it seems solid seems  fine  it's not really susceptible to  temperature or humidity changes but it  is wood it moves and therefore it can  break unfortunately i've had many cracks  in some of my favorite instruments and  it's a real pain so to spare you that  trouble uh this video is going to be all  about why you need to humidify your  guitar  for many years i worked in a music store  that had a really good guitar repair  shop and i even learned to do a couple  good guitar repairs myself but i'll tell  you every single year at the end of  winter we'd have an influx of dozens and  dozens of people coming in saying hey i  don't know what's wrong my guitar is all  buzzy or hey my my bridge is coming off  my instrument or my bridge came off the  instrument or i have a crack in the top  of my guitar what happened i don't know  what i don't know what the problem is  and the problem always was that that  person didn't humidify their guitar now  i'm in the midwest so we have super dry  super cold winters so depending on where  you are this might be relative to your  climate but essentially the rule is if  you're below 40 or above 60 humidity you  have to be a little bit worried about  the longevity and the safety of your  instruments and luckily it's actually  pretty easy and affordable to make sure  that this doesn't happen to you so  essentially what happens is that wood  contracts or expands depending on the  amount of moisture in the air and if  your guitar was built to be in that  window of 40 to 60 humidity which is  about normal  and suddenly it's exposed to 20 humidity  that radical shock can make the wood  crack and even if the wood doesn't crack  what often happens on guitars is that  when the instrument gets super dry and  the wood contracts the bridge sinks down  a little bit and that means that the  strings sink down a little bit  causing the instrument to be suddenly  buzzy when it wasn't before other things  that might happen is while the wood is  changing size the glue can break and  maybe the bridge comes off sometimes the  top rips off or the braces on inside the  sound hole might come loose and cause  buzzing so i hope now i've scared you  enough to want to humidify your guitar  luckily this is pretty easy and cost  affordable so let's go through some  quick ways that you guys can be  humidifying and protecting your  instruments  now one of the first things you can do  is have a solid case now this is  actually my baroque guitar case so it's  a little bit smaller but it's the same  idea there are cases for your  instruments or guitars that have a wood  lining inside and so this is a really  good way to trap the humidity inside the  instrument and create a little  quarantined area that is very safe  a gig bag is too flimsy it won't it  won't hold in the humidity but a hard  shell case  will now inside i have these little  packs humapax humidifying packs these  are by a company called boveda  i'm not sponsored by them i just use  these because they work really well  there's a lot of companies that make  little packs like this you can put right  inside your your case  this is the same company who makes uh  huma packs for cigar boxes and things um  and on the label it says 49  uh two-way humidifier so why i love  these is because basically you put these  in your case and you're done if there's  too much moisture it'll  suck the moisture out and get it back to  49 if there's too little moisture and  it's too dry it'll it'll regulate it  back to 49 so whether it's too humid or  not humid enough it gets you back to 49  i have two in the case just to make sure  it does the job  uh and it seems to so these are really  cool and easy to use i bought a bunch of  these and i have them in all my cases  it's also super important that you then  leave your case closed this only works  if you have a quarantined environment  but then your case is always this  beautifully 49 preserved humidity in  there  so leave the guitar in its case when  you're not using it and have the hemopax  in there some companies also have  hemopacts that go in these little cloth  bags that even drop in between the  strings so they drop in the sound hole  that's another good option i might even  have another one in the headstock  compartment just to make sure that that  case is totally quarantined for a diy  solution  my luthier friends the brene showed me  this little trick they took a film  canister cut some holes in it and on the  inside there is a sponge so you just  have to wet the sponge  and then put in the film canister and  then throw that in your case  just make sure the sponge isn't dripping  water everywhere but in this one you  have to refill the sponge uh sometimes  once a day  if it's very dry  then some other companies like planet  waves make this one which i used for a  long time  this is  a the same idea as the film canister  but this is made to drop down between  your strings so that goes right in there  and this is basically the same thing you  open it up  and on the inside there is a  sponge but i really had to fill this up  so often i love the huma packs because  you just throw it in there and you  forget about it  i also found these very helpful this is  a digital hygrometer a hygrometer  measures the humidity in the air it also  tells me the temperature and i have the  one of these in my office and i also  have a few of these in some of my cases  um but a little warning uh digital  hygrometers are known to be  pretty inaccurate  sometimes more than ten percent uh i  went to a luthier who had a really good  um actual i don't know if you call it an  analog hygrometer but one that was  calibrated and worked and i just  measured it to this and this was 10 low  and so now i know it's just a little  it's usually 10 lower than the actual  humidity but it's in the ballpark  and it's better to know a general idea  of where you are so if this thing ever  says you know 20  or 15 percent i know it's very dry and  it seems to be pretty accurate for that  now here in my home office my solution  is to actually just have a humidifier  going in my room so that when the  instruments are out they're also you  know at a nice humidity and for that i  have this big guy  just a big tower which i fill up with  water and then it mists out of the top  and within a few hours of this thing  running i can easily get the room to 50  so like i said i really do consider this  a public service announcement please do  yourself a favor and humidify your  precious guitars so thanks so much for  watching i really hope you found this  tip useful i have lots of other guitar  tips you can check out here on youtube  and if you want to see a lot of extra  guitar tips which are only available for  my patrons head over to my patreon where  you can do a very minimal pledge per  month and get access to a lot of behind  the scenes content extra digital  downloads and insight into the classical  guitar course that i'm working on  so stay tuned for more and i'll see you  guys next time  [Music]  you

